WEBVTT

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.638
["Let Me Go" playing]

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.231
[man] I'm Gurki's gay best friend.

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:27.110
I think that Gurki can sometimes
intimidate men

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.321
because she is just such
a stunning, confident woman.

00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:34.075
♪ You made plans and I
I made... ♪

00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:36.411
[woman] From Gurki, I learned that men
can be shitty.

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:39.873
It is hard to be a smart,
single woman in New York City.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.543
♪ And I wished you had hurt me harder... ♪

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:45.378
[man] I think Gurki
does have some walls up,

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:48.131
but those walls come from a place
of self-protection.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.845
♪ And I wish you wouldn't wait for me
But you always do ♪

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:56.306
♪ I've been hoping ♪

00:00:56.431 --> 00:01:01.061
♪ Somebody loves you
In the ways I couldn't ♪

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:05.732
♪ Somebody's taking care of
All of the mess I made ♪

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:07.901
♪ Someone you don't have to change ♪

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.195
♪ I've been hoping ♪

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:13.323
♪ Someone will love you
Let me go ♪

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.159
-Hi!
-How's it going?

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.410
Good.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:20.789
-You're making it up that mountain.
-This is the steepest ramp over here.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:21.706
Are you Salim?

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.124
I am Salim. What is your name?

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.667
-Gurki.
-What is it?

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:25.710
-Gurki.
-Gurki?

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:27.170
-Yes.
-I'm Bam. Nice to meet you.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.713
-Bam?
-Yeah, like the cartoon.

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:30.381
-Nice to meet you.
-You look beautiful!

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:32.801
-Thank you! You look handsome.
-Like a special occasion.

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:33.843
Yeah. [chuckles]

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.386
-You ready for a drink?
-I am.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.471
-Yeah.
-Ladies first.

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:37.430
Thank you.

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:44.771
Well, I think I may do a Prosecco.

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.607
That sounds good. I usually get rosé,
but I'll do that too.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.192
-Two Proseccos, please.
-[man] Okay.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:51.069
[man] I'll do a Miller High Life.

00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:53.279
Miller High Life? The champagne of beers.

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:55.281
"The champagne of beers."
You know what's up.

00:01:55.365 --> 00:01:56.783
-Well, I'm from Texas.
-Okay.

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:58.952
That's, like, beer country,
so I know my beers.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:00.495
-How is New York?
-New York is good.

00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:02.997
-How long?
-A little over two years.

00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:04.958
-Oh, so you're brand new.
-Yeah.

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:06.876
-How long have you been here?
-A little over 13.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.130
Oh, my God. So you're, like, a legit
New Yorker. 'Cause ten years, they say

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.924
-you can call yourself one.
-You get your card. I never got mine yet,

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:15.093
-but cheers.
-Cheers.

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:18.555
So you go by Bam, but your mom
didn't, like, name you Bam.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:19.889
No. It's actually Roberto.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:22.183
I was always in front of the TV
watching The Flintstones,

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:23.560
-so my first word--
-I wondered.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.520
"I wonder if it has to do
with The Flintstones."

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:28.231
It was the first word I ever said.
It wasn't "mom" or "dad."

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.441
-It was "bam."
-So, where is your family from?

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:32.819
-We are of, like, Puerto Rican descent.
-Oh, cool.

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:34.028
Where are you from?

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:36.781
My parents are originally from
the state called Punjab in India,

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:38.575
which is north... North India.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.368
I was actually born in France.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:41.661
-Born in France?
-Yes.

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:43.746
-How'd that happen?
-I know, It's a weird story.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.875
So, my dad, he was, like, backpacking
across Europe with a friend,

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:50.503
ran out of money and just decided
to join the French Foreign Legion.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:51.671
-Okay.
-Then, my parents

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:53.423
had an arranged marriage,
so they never met.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.258
-They met on their wedding day.
-Arrangement.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.801
They're still married and everything.

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:59.804
-Tu parles français?
-Je parle français. Vouz parlez aussi?

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:00.763
Non.

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:03.474
-That's pretty good!
-Yeah, that's about all I know.

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:05.101
-Not bad.
-I took it in high school.

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:08.980
-What do you do for work?
-I started a beverage company.

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.482
I actually work
for a start-up tech company.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.067
-That's awesome.
-My friend told me,

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:16.613
"Hey, get your real estate license,"
and joined a really amazing team.

00:03:16.946 --> 00:03:17.864
I'm a SWAT officer.

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:19.908
So that's, like,
when some shit's going down.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:21.576
-Yeah.
-[Gurki giggles]

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:23.369
What made you wanna join...

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:27.624
I come from a line of first responders.
So my father was a correctional officer,

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.043
and his brother was
a New York City police officer,

00:03:30.126 --> 00:03:33.296
-so it was kind of a rite of passage.
-That's cool. You're living your dream.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.131
-Yeah.
-That's awesome.

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:36.215
-You?
-Good for you.

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:39.135
I'm a buyer for jewelry
for Barneys New York,

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.304
-so... I have an MBA.
-Hmm.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:44.474
So I'm very, like, business-oriented.
I like numbers and all of that.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:46.851
And luckily, I feel like
I'm pretty good at it,

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:48.770
I have a great team that I work with.

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:51.397
Doing something you love
in New York is, like, rare.

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:54.275
When you can find the thing you're
passionate about, you never look back.

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:57.654
Never look back, no. I'm not
a looking-back kind of girl anyway.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.698
So, you're, like, representing New York.
You've Statue of Liberty--

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:03.701
Yeah. One of the most fondest memories
of me as a child is

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:06.162
my mother taking my brother and I
to Ellis Island.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:08.581
I just remember being
in awe of Lady Liberty.

00:04:08.665 --> 00:04:11.501
So there's, like, five points
which represent the five boroughs.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.419
-I actually didn't know that.
-Yeah.

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:15.880
-That's an interesting fact.
-A New Yorker should know that.

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.340
[Gurki]
Well, I have one tattoo.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:18.633
Mine is just right here.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:19.968
[Jay] Okay. All right.

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:22.679
-What's that about?
-Uh, it's from my parents' religion.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:23.972
-Okay.
-So, they're Sikh,

00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:28.184
and it means we're all one race sort of
thing, regardless of where you're from.

00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:29.352
Very interesting.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.105
Have you been to... India?

00:04:32.188 --> 00:04:34.649
I have, yeah. Last time I went
was, like, six years ago.

00:04:34.732 --> 00:04:36.025
-Six years? Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:39.570
I actually lived in Israel
for about two years almost...

00:04:39.654 --> 00:04:41.406
-Oh, cool.
-...from 2008 to 2010.

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:43.992
-Do you know that show Fauza or--
-Fauda.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:46.119
Yeah! I watched it! It's so good.

00:04:46.202 --> 00:04:48.121
-Everyone tells me to watch it.
-It's so good.

00:04:48.204 --> 00:04:49.580
I did see the first episode.

00:04:49.747 --> 00:04:53.001
I liked it because there's the whole
krav maga, because I've been learning it,

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.669
-so--
-I don't know what that means.

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:57.630
Krav maga, that's the martial art
that they... that they use.

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:00.675
-It's a very Israeli... It's very self--
-Show me a move.

00:05:00.925 --> 00:05:01.759
[chuckles]

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:05.305
-Show me, like, how does it start.
-Okay. Let's do, like...

00:05:05.388 --> 00:05:07.348
-Okay, if someone grabs you, choke...
-Okay.

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:08.891
Two arms around the neck and then...

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:10.852
there's a basic move
where you hold here.

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:12.895
-Uh-huh.
-And then you bring your arm over,

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:13.813
and then here...

00:05:13.896 --> 00:05:15.481
-Okay.
-Then you throw an elbow...

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.693
-It's a quick one, two, and then a knee.
-So it's like...

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:20.194
-It's all self-defense.
-Yes.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.571
-Gotcha.
-There's a lot of,

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:24.741
you know, defense from knife attacks,
a lot of gun defense.

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.493
-How old are you? Do you mind me asking?
-I am 27.

00:05:27.577 --> 00:05:29.037
Oh, my God. You're young!

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.040
You seem mature. You don't seem
like you're some crazy 20-year-old

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:33.332
-or anything.
-Thank you.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:34.876
You hear that? I'm mature.

00:05:35.543 --> 00:05:37.962
You're young.
I thought you were, like, older than 30.

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:39.213
-Yeah.
-I'm 36.

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.424
Yeah, I'm actually... Never mind.
You look great.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:42.717
Oh, thank you.

00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:44.886
-You do not look 36.
-I am 36.

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:45.803
-Okay.
-Yes.

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:47.347
I got the gray hairs to prove it.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.682
-They're tucked in here.
-I got the grays come in too.

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:50.683
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.101
I actually turned 34 on Sunday.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:54.312
Oh, happy birthday! Well, cheers.

00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:55.646
Cheers. Thank you.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.692
-Um, should we sit down and eat or--
-Sure. Absolutely.

00:05:59.776 --> 00:06:01.778
Okay. Cool. Let's do that.

00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:11.579
I will just...
as gracefully as possible...

00:06:12.121 --> 00:06:13.623
climb over this bench.

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.708
[woman]
Can I get you guys a drink to start with?

00:06:15.792 --> 00:06:18.044
I'm gonna have a vodka seltzer.

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:20.213
-And for you?
-I'm gonna stick to rosé.

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:21.297
-Rosé?
-Yeah.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.840
She's Rosé All Day.

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:27.095
-Uh, I'm gonna go Bulleit on the rocks.
-Bulleit on the rocks. Okay.

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:29.263
That whole "Rosé All Day" thing

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:31.682
-drives me fucking crazy.
-I know.

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:33.559
Like, people have T-shirts that say

00:06:33.643 --> 00:06:36.938
-"Rosé all day." I'm like, "Can we just--"
-But the kids now,

00:06:37.021 --> 00:06:40.525
I say this because it's bullshit that
technically I'm still called a millennial,

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:42.276
but it's the things they come up with,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.945
like "on fleek" or "lit"

00:06:44.028 --> 00:06:46.280
or all these millions of words, like--

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:48.616
Although I love saying "on fleek,"
like, as a joke.

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:52.829
You know, I'm like, "Man, my presen--"
"I kicked ass. I was on fucking fleek."

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.372
Yeah, exactly. Wow.

00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:57.583
So, that's a nice little leak here
hanging out with us.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.669
[thunder rumbles]

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:01.421
[Gurki] I'm eating everything.

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:03.047
-Well--
-You don't have to eat.

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:05.216
Don't feel pressured to eat
because I said that.

00:07:05.299 --> 00:07:06.592
I've to kind of catch up now.

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:09.345
I'm warning you that if you don't eat,
it's gonna be gone,

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:10.513
'cause I'm gonna eat it all.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:13.057
-Listen, sharing is caring in my book.
-[Gurki laughs]

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.644
What is top on your bucket list?

00:07:17.562 --> 00:07:18.563
I don't have one.

00:07:18.646 --> 00:07:21.149
You don't have a bucket list?
You've never even thought...

00:07:21.232 --> 00:07:22.233
-Really?
-No.

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:23.860
Bucket list means death.

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:25.820
-Do you?
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:28.781
-What's on your bucket list?
-I've a long bucket list. Space.

00:07:28.865 --> 00:07:30.992
-I wanna go to space.
-You wanna go to space?

00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:32.952
-I wanna go to space.
-That's crazy.

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.291
[Gurki] So, if you weren't, like,
doing what you're doing now,

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.294
like, what... If you could do
whatever you want, what would you do?

00:07:41.377 --> 00:07:43.337
Is this like if... like just a wildcard,

00:07:43.421 --> 00:07:44.714
-do whatever I want?
-Totally.

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.425
Oh, I'd be onstage every night
with a guitar in my hand.

00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:50.094
-Oh, nice.You play the guitar?
-No, I don't.

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:51.762
[laughs]

00:07:53.181 --> 00:07:55.016
So, how is it dating as, like, a cop?

00:07:55.391 --> 00:07:56.684
-Ooph!
-[Gurki laughs]

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:59.937
-It's tough. It's not easy by any means.
-Dang! You went "Ooph."

00:08:00.021 --> 00:08:02.523
Just because we've certain constraints
that, like...

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:05.318
You know, we're not allowed to associate
with known felons.

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:09.155
What kind of girls are you finding
that are felons? [laughs]

00:08:10.448 --> 00:08:12.116
On a non-serious note,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.286
do you have any, like,
weird pet peeves in, like, relationships?

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:16.662
-Cats.
-[chuckles]

00:08:16.746 --> 00:08:18.664
-You hate cats?
-Uh, cats are the devil.

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:21.083
-[giggles]
-The girl that I dated,

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.045
she had this cat
she inherited from an ex-boyfriend,

00:08:24.128 --> 00:08:27.215
which was weird to me to begin with.
So, like, we'd be sleeping,

00:08:27.298 --> 00:08:30.718
and her bedroom door had a crack
at the bottom, like, this big, right?

00:08:30.801 --> 00:08:32.637
And then it would crawl underneath.

00:08:32.803 --> 00:08:35.765
I thought, "I'm gonna outsmart this cat.
I'm not gonna be fooled by a cat."

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:40.186
So then I put pillows under there. I'm
gonna jam one of her high heels in there.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:44.106
And there's no way a toddler could get
this... this out of here. There's no way.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.193
-No, cats are fucking ridiculously...
-That freaking cat figured it out.

00:08:47.276 --> 00:08:48.778
-...agile.
-Then I said to her,

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:51.489
"Listen, it's either me or the cat."
She got rid of the cat.

00:08:51.572 --> 00:08:53.824
She broke with up you...
No, she got rid of the cat.

00:08:53.908 --> 00:08:56.536
Stayed with me. Come on.
I ain't gonna lose to a cat.

00:08:56.619 --> 00:08:58.454
Are you crazy? Get out of here.

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:00.540
-No, I just...  That's a deal breaker--
-Okay, wait!

00:09:00.623 --> 00:09:04.001
So, you made her get rid of her cat,
and then you broke up with her.

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:07.296
Well... it's a deeper story
than that, but...

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.674
-Okay. Let's hear it.
-We... we... Uh.

00:09:09.757 --> 00:09:12.635
Basically, we dated for two years,
we lived together,

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:16.430
and I was nowhere near ready
to be a father or a husband.

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:19.934
I knew it wasn't fair to keep dragging
that on, so that didn't work out.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:24.564
Probably my only serious relationship,
the longest relationship.

00:09:24.647 --> 00:09:26.148
-Mm-hmm.
-Two and a half years.

00:09:26.232 --> 00:09:29.610
But it was long-distance
really the whole time, which is not easy.

00:09:29.986 --> 00:09:31.612
[Bam]
She really is an amazing person.

00:09:31.696 --> 00:09:35.032
It's just... she didn't understand
what it is to have a really close family.

00:09:35.116 --> 00:09:38.536
Like, once she saw how close
I was with my family, it just, like...

00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:42.081
-Yeah.
-She didn't know how to react to it.

00:09:42.164 --> 00:09:45.209
What about you?
I see you asking me a lot of questions...

00:09:45.293 --> 00:09:47.545
-What about me?
-...about my profession...

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.548
So, like, your last relationship,
what was that about?

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:53.050
-So, I'm actually divorced.
-Wow!

00:09:53.134 --> 00:09:55.845
Which is different for me.
I've never experienced that.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.432
I was also married for a while
when I was younger.

00:09:59.724 --> 00:10:00.600
Oh, me too.

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:02.184
-Really? I--
-I'm divorced, yes.

00:10:02.268 --> 00:10:04.895
That's interesting. I was...
Love hearing that commonality.

00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:08.399
Uh, yeah, I was...
I was very young, though.

00:10:08.482 --> 00:10:09.942
Wait, let me tell you the thing

00:10:10.026 --> 00:10:13.529
that most people tell you when
they say you're divorced, "I'm so sorry."

00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:14.447
-Of course.
-Yeah.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.324
-"I'm so sorry."
-You shouldn't be too sorry.

00:10:16.407 --> 00:10:18.659
"Actually, it's the best thing
that ever happened to me,

00:10:18.743 --> 00:10:21.787
but thanks anyway for your sympathy."

00:10:21.871 --> 00:10:24.165
You know what?
No one really knows what to say.

00:10:24.248 --> 00:10:26.292
I don't know what to say
to another divorcee.

00:10:26.375 --> 00:10:28.336
I wanna high five. "What up?
Welcome to the club."

00:10:28.753 --> 00:10:30.630
-"You're free!"
-Yeah!

00:10:31.672 --> 00:10:34.050
But honestly, I have nothing bad
to say about my ex-wife.

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:36.510
We get along well
because I also have a daughter.

00:10:36.594 --> 00:10:39.096
That must be, like, part of
why you seem so mature.

00:10:39.347 --> 00:10:42.224
-Yes.
-I didn't pinpoint you for in your 20s.

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.185
When you said 27, I was like, "What?

00:10:44.602 --> 00:10:46.479
-How?"
-[laughs] I get that. I'll...

00:10:46.562 --> 00:10:48.189
Thank you. I appreciate that.

00:10:48.272 --> 00:10:50.775
-How young were you when you got married?
-Twenty one.

00:10:50.858 --> 00:10:54.070
-Oh, shit.
-21 when it was all really unfolding.

00:10:54.153 --> 00:10:56.447
Yeah. That's even more, like,
younger than me.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:58.658
Like, I was with my ex-husband.

00:10:58.741 --> 00:11:01.285
He was my first boyfriend, so we were 17.

00:11:01.369 --> 00:11:03.621
-Geez.
-We didn't get married till I was 25.

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:06.290
Then I got divorced
by the time I was your age, 30,

00:11:06.374 --> 00:11:07.875
-and I'm now 36.
-Okay.

00:11:07.958 --> 00:11:09.960
So I've been doing it literally backwards.

00:11:10.044 --> 00:11:13.172
My friends joke that I'm Benjamin Button.
Is that the name?

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:15.132
Benjamin Button, yes.
Which is a good problem.

00:11:15.216 --> 00:11:17.551
-'Cause I'm living life backwards.
-That's all right.

00:11:17.635 --> 00:11:21.680
-Yeah. I've been single for a hot second.
-Hot second? That's a hot minute.

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:23.432
-Not even a hot second.
-Yeah, a hot week.

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:25.184
[both laughing]

00:11:25.309 --> 00:11:26.143
But, um...

00:11:26.227 --> 00:11:27.770
-[Jay] Oh, yeah.
-[laughs]

00:11:29.146 --> 00:11:30.898
[thunder rumbling]

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:36.570
You said you were married. My parents
got divorced when I was 23 years old,

00:11:36.654 --> 00:11:38.614
-and I was living here by myself.
-Really?

00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:40.241
-It was shocking?
-It was shocking.

00:11:40.324 --> 00:11:42.284
You think it will never happen to you,

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:43.953
-then it does.
-Why did they get divorced?

00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:45.496
They were married for 23 years.

00:11:45.579 --> 00:11:48.374
They got married when I...
They were 21 years old.

00:11:48.666 --> 00:11:52.086
-I saw them go through good and bad--
-My parents met on their wedding day.

00:11:52.503 --> 00:11:54.004
They had an arranged marriage.

00:11:54.463 --> 00:11:55.965
-Isn't that wild?
-Still together?

00:11:56.048 --> 00:11:57.341
Yeah, they're still together.

00:11:57.425 --> 00:12:00.094
They came from a culture
where that was, like, normal.

00:12:00.719 --> 00:12:02.722
-They had no--
-They don't have, like, a...

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:06.016
They're not, like, wildly in love now
at this point,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.393
-but--
-That's terrible.

00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:10.813
Is it terrible? I don't know.
They have me and my brother.

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:12.815
I want to be madly in love until I'm 85.

00:12:12.898 --> 00:12:14.400
No, totally. I feel the same way.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:18.487
But how do you feel that it's like...
like, a woman or a man has no choice in--

00:12:18.571 --> 00:12:21.824
Arranged meaning they got to pick
who they wanted to marry, but--

00:12:21.907 --> 00:12:23.617
You said they met
on their wedding day.

00:12:23.701 --> 00:12:24.827
But they saw a picture.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:28.747
A picture. So they basically did
a dating app and were like, "I'm all in."

00:12:28.998 --> 00:12:32.334
Oh, my God. I never thought about that.
My parents basically, like,

00:12:32.418 --> 00:12:35.129
-Tindered and got married.
-They swiped. Sold!

00:12:36.464 --> 00:12:37.673
I don't know.

00:12:37.757 --> 00:12:38.966
-That's amazing.
-That's scary.

00:12:39.049 --> 00:12:41.302
-I cannot wait to tell my brother that.
-And they had kids.

00:12:41.385 --> 00:12:42.219
Yeah.

00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:43.512
So, was yours arranged?

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:46.599
No. It was somebody I dated in college.

00:12:46.682 --> 00:12:49.769
Like, he was Punjabi,
which is where my family is from,

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.480
and I just knew him, whatever.
So we dated.

00:12:52.688 --> 00:12:55.232
Um, but it was...
it was definitely not arranged.

00:12:55.316 --> 00:12:59.153
And how long did you know that it was
not gonna be forever?

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:01.864
Ugh! Really early on, sadly.

00:13:03.032 --> 00:13:05.451
-[chuckling] I know.
-You knew right away?

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:07.953
It was more when we started dating.

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:11.332
It was very taboo for, like,
people to be dating, and, like,

00:13:11.665 --> 00:13:13.793
not have marriage as the end goal.

00:13:15.377 --> 00:13:18.422
Like, so then you're like,
"Well, it seems kind of petty

00:13:18.506 --> 00:13:20.466
to, like, not get married to this guy

00:13:20.549 --> 00:13:23.511
that you've been dating for a long time
for, like, no reason."

00:13:23.594 --> 00:13:26.514
Your parents made it work.
You should be able to make it work.

00:13:26.847 --> 00:13:30.434
But we just weren't, like, the best
of ourselves with each other,

00:13:30.518 --> 00:13:32.102
and I feel when you're in a relationship,

00:13:32.186 --> 00:13:35.022
like, you wanna be the best of yourself
for the other person.

00:13:35.272 --> 00:13:37.441
But, like, I also wasn't happy
in the relationship.

00:13:37.525 --> 00:13:40.778
To be honest, I was kind of a shitty wife
and significant other too,

00:13:40.861 --> 00:13:43.239
'cause I didn't know how to be,
like, a good partner.

00:13:43.322 --> 00:13:46.200
-I was a kid when we got together.
-You know what? The fact that you said,

00:13:46.283 --> 00:13:48.911
"I was a shitty wife or a shitty partner"
or whatever...

00:13:49.495 --> 00:13:51.455
that's huge
'cause most people won't say that.

00:13:51.539 --> 00:13:53.290
They'd point the finger
and be, "That guy."

00:13:53.624 --> 00:13:55.209
-Yeah.
-"He messed up. It wasn't me.

00:13:56.001 --> 00:13:56.836
It was him."

00:13:57.294 --> 00:13:58.420
So that's like...

00:13:58.796 --> 00:14:03.551
To me, that speaks volumes
that you're able to sit there and say,

00:14:03.634 --> 00:14:06.929
"Maybe I could've been a better wife
or a partner, whatever the case may be."

00:14:07.847 --> 00:14:09.431
Most people don't have that in them.

00:14:11.058 --> 00:14:12.101
So, wait a minute.

00:14:12.184 --> 00:14:14.144
You walked down the aisle
in your white dress.

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:17.982
I'm sure Indian weddings are a big to-do.
I've seen, like... I know what's up.

00:14:18.399 --> 00:14:20.651
Like, you were walking down the aisle
and you're like...

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:22.069
Well...

00:14:22.152 --> 00:14:24.530
No, tell the truth. Come on.
Don't "Well."

00:14:25.447 --> 00:14:27.408
-You're fucking calling me out.
-Yeah. Say it.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:33.497
Well, no.
There was a lot of pressure, for sure,

00:14:33.581 --> 00:14:36.458
and, like, obviously, like,
my family wanted to make it happen--

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:38.252
You went through with something like that?

00:14:38.335 --> 00:14:40.504
Because I've loved girls,
but I wouldn't want to...

00:14:41.005 --> 00:14:43.340
go down the aisle
in front of my friends and family.

00:14:43.424 --> 00:14:46.176
But I get the pressure.
I know what you're saying.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:48.429
Who said they were getting a divorce,
you or him?

00:14:48.512 --> 00:14:50.890
The final straw was, like,
he, like, cheated,

00:14:51.181 --> 00:14:52.474
which is like, "Okay."

00:14:53.017 --> 00:14:55.936
-That's, like, my deal breaker.
-No, I get that.

00:14:57.062 --> 00:14:59.565
[thunder rumbling]

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:05.779
[Justin] Let's go back inside--

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.033
-I feel like we're gonna get rained on.
-I'm starting to get wet already.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:12.703
[Gurki] Aah!

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.288
Lots of rain, let's find cover.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:17.625
[Justin] Okay. Yeah.

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:22.796
[Gurki] This place will have more drinks,

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:26.467
-which you don't drink as much, but--
-After you, my lady. Lead the way.

00:15:26.967 --> 00:15:28.969
And, yeah, I do not drink as much,

00:15:29.053 --> 00:15:31.680
but I'm definitely a fan
of the potential...

00:15:31.889 --> 00:15:35.225
-[chortles]
-...dance moves that we can be having.

00:15:35.309 --> 00:15:36.769
Oh, my God. What was that?

00:15:36.852 --> 00:15:39.980
-What was that little leg thing?
-It's a little skip-a-dee-doo.

00:15:40.064 --> 00:15:42.399
-You just--
-Okay. So you just do that when?

00:15:42.483 --> 00:15:43.609
-Randomly?
-Randomly.

00:15:43.692 --> 00:15:46.737
There's also a walk you could do where
you cross over your legs and you just...

00:15:47.821 --> 00:15:49.198
[Gurki laughs]

00:15:49.448 --> 00:15:52.326
-[Salim] I'm gonna step to this side.
-Why?

00:15:52.409 --> 00:15:54.078
Ah, just used to doing that.

00:15:54.161 --> 00:15:57.915
It's the gentleman thing, stand
between you and the road, just in case.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:15:59.917
-[Gurki] That's very nice.
-[Salim] Yes.

00:16:00.167 --> 00:16:02.378
What if there's a crazy person
on this side?

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:05.589
-Then what happens?
-Well, then I'd have to switch.

00:16:05.673 --> 00:16:07.633
-Then you'd have to improvise?
-Yes, yes.

00:16:07.716 --> 00:16:09.426
-You make do, you make do.
-Yeah.

00:16:12.846 --> 00:16:14.682
[Jay]
Hopefully we can do this again soon.

00:16:14.765 --> 00:16:15.974
[Gurki] Yeah, maybe.

00:16:16.058 --> 00:16:18.143
[Gurki] I have your Instagram handle.

00:16:18.227 --> 00:16:19.353
-[Jay] Okay.
-[Gurki laughs]

00:16:19.436 --> 00:16:21.939
-[Jay] DM me or something like that, okay?
-[Gurki] Sounds good.

00:16:22.815 --> 00:16:24.692
[Bam] So, we'll definitely do this again

00:16:24.775 --> 00:16:27.736
and... next time, we'll be more...

00:16:28.404 --> 00:16:30.072
-uh, active...
-Cool.

00:16:30.155 --> 00:16:31.740
...than eating all day.
-Yeah.

00:16:32.032 --> 00:16:33.701
-It was nice to meet you.
-Yes.

00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:36.453
-I'm gonna call a Lyft.
-All right. It was a pleasure.

00:16:37.037 --> 00:16:39.289
-And we'll talk soon.
-Sounds good. See you.

00:16:41.375 --> 00:16:43.460
[Gurki] This is awesome lighting.
Let's take a picture.

00:16:43.961 --> 00:16:46.338
-[Jay] You got to the right side, right?
-[Gurki] Yeah.

00:16:46.755 --> 00:16:48.048
This is my side.

00:16:49.591 --> 00:16:50.634
[thunder rumbles]

00:16:50.718 --> 00:16:53.095
Before it rains! Oh, it's coming.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:55.055
-Oh, no.
-All right. Let's take one.

00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:56.974
[thunder rumbling]

00:16:57.057 --> 00:16:58.809
Okay. Now I'm running for it.

00:16:59.309 --> 00:17:00.644
[thunder crashing]

00:17:00.728 --> 00:17:01.937
[Gurki] Oh, my gosh!

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:02.896
Shit!

00:17:03.772 --> 00:17:04.898
See you, Jay.

00:17:10.154 --> 00:17:11.864
-[Justin] Whoo! Let's go.
-[Gurki] Oh, God!

00:17:14.825 --> 00:17:15.659
All right.

00:17:16.201 --> 00:17:17.995
-Okay. That's not open.
-[laughs]

00:17:18.078 --> 00:17:20.330
-Ladies first.
-Thank you.

00:17:20.831 --> 00:17:22.124
[Justin] Oh, to be dry!

00:17:22.916 --> 00:17:25.210
[Gurki] All right.
Well, this place is fancy.

00:17:25.294 --> 00:17:27.046
[Salim] It's very fancy.

00:17:30.215 --> 00:17:32.259
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:17:33.469 --> 00:17:35.512
-Do you like to dance?
-Oh, man.

00:17:35.596 --> 00:17:36.472
[Gurki giggles]

00:17:36.555 --> 00:17:37.806
Dancing, yeah.

00:17:38.182 --> 00:17:39.892
I'd say I'm a step above terrible.

00:17:40.309 --> 00:17:43.020
-[chuckles]
-Step above terrible and improving.

00:17:43.353 --> 00:17:44.646
Do you like to dance?

00:17:44.855 --> 00:17:47.733
I love to dance. I'm also terrible,
but I don't give a shit.

00:17:47.816 --> 00:17:49.568
-I feel you're being...
-I love to dance.

00:17:49.651 --> 00:17:51.070
I feel you're a great dancer.

00:17:51.361 --> 00:17:53.280
No, I'm actually really not.

00:17:53.697 --> 00:17:56.492
So, yeah, here's the deal.
You snap, I'll dance.

00:17:57.367 --> 00:17:59.411
-Is that fair? I'll show you--
-What does snap mean?

00:18:00.954 --> 00:18:02.456
Like, Snapchat? Oh, okay.

00:18:02.539 --> 00:18:03.415
[Manny] Oh, my gosh!

00:18:03.707 --> 00:18:04.875
You millennial, you.

00:18:05.375 --> 00:18:07.503
Can you show me an Indian move?
I will do it.

00:18:07.586 --> 00:18:09.463
I just want to see what's the step.

00:18:09.546 --> 00:18:10.380
Is it...

00:18:10.839 --> 00:18:12.299
-Oh, my God.
-Give me something.

00:18:12.841 --> 00:18:14.176
-All right. Fine.
-Yeah.

00:18:14.426 --> 00:18:15.385
I'm trying to think.

00:18:15.594 --> 00:18:17.054
There's this thing where it's like...

00:18:17.554 --> 00:18:19.681
like this and then you just...

00:18:20.182 --> 00:18:21.767
-[Manny] Kick the feet out.
-[Gurki] Yep.

00:18:22.142 --> 00:18:24.061
-Bam.
-No, but your hands.

00:18:24.144 --> 00:18:26.605
So you have to have one hand up
and one hand down.

00:18:26.688 --> 00:18:27.981
-Okay.
-Then you just go like...

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:31.026
Pretend you're, like, my...
my girlfriend in the village,

00:18:31.401 --> 00:18:34.822
-and you're just dancing in the field.
-That's what you're doing?

00:18:34.905 --> 00:18:37.574
-Exactly. It's a girl thing!
-You've gathered the wheat stalks,

00:18:37.658 --> 00:18:39.493
and now you put them down.
You do a quick move,

00:18:39.576 --> 00:18:41.620
and you're like, "I hope someone sees.

00:18:42.579 --> 00:18:44.748
-Is my future husband watching?"
-Yeah. Exactly.

00:18:45.582 --> 00:18:46.750
You have two siblings.

00:18:46.834 --> 00:18:49.169
-We're 18 months between each.
-That's intense.

00:18:49.253 --> 00:18:50.129
Crazy, yeah.

00:18:50.212 --> 00:18:53.006
-Do you want kids?
-Yes, definitely.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:54.633
I want kids for sure.

00:18:54.716 --> 00:18:56.009
I'm the oldest of four.

00:18:56.468 --> 00:18:59.388
There's nothing more intriguing to me
than being a father.

00:18:59.471 --> 00:19:00.305
Yeah.

00:19:00.556 --> 00:19:03.058
Are you, like, wanting to have more kids?

00:19:03.142 --> 00:19:05.561
-Do you want to focus on your daughter?
-I do--

00:19:05.644 --> 00:19:07.813
-Are you not worried about it?
-I'd love to have...

00:19:08.856 --> 00:19:10.399
a family, uh...

00:19:11.358 --> 00:19:14.528
you know, with someone
that I choose to be with.

00:19:15.279 --> 00:19:16.572
Do you want to get married again?

00:19:17.239 --> 00:19:18.907
Marriage is a beautiful thing,

00:19:18.991 --> 00:19:22.661
and if you find the right person,
and it works out, it's great and...

00:19:22.953 --> 00:19:24.705
um, and then with kids, like...

00:19:25.998 --> 00:19:28.750
-I don't know if I want kids, honestly.
-Interesting.

00:19:28.834 --> 00:19:31.128
Yeah. Like, I would love to have kids,

00:19:31.211 --> 00:19:34.923
but I'm also 36,
and, like, from a realistic perspective...

00:19:36.091 --> 00:19:38.969
I don't know. It would be nice,
but I'm going to be happy with--

00:19:39.052 --> 00:19:41.471
-Whatever happens, happens.
-Exactly, exactly.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.057
It's a give and take,
this is what I've learned

00:19:44.141 --> 00:19:45.851
about relationships is that...

00:19:46.476 --> 00:19:47.769
it's not about you.

00:19:47.853 --> 00:19:51.398
When you find that person,
you're letting a piece of yourself go.

00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:52.983
That's not easy to do.

00:19:53.108 --> 00:19:56.320
-Oh, my God. I don't believe that at all.
-You don't believe that?

00:19:56.403 --> 00:20:00.073
No. I think if you find the right person,
you're not letting anything go.

00:20:00.157 --> 00:20:04.119
-You're with somebody that accentuates--
-You've not lived here long enough.

00:20:05.287 --> 00:20:07.956
You...
I don't think you've been in love, then.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.416
-I've been in love.
-I honestly...

00:20:10.083 --> 00:20:10.959
No, man. When you're--

00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:13.837
Because when you're in love,
you put the other person first.

00:20:14.087 --> 00:20:14.922
And now--

00:20:15.005 --> 00:20:17.883
That doesn't mean
a part of you disappears. You're just--

00:20:17.966 --> 00:20:20.135
How do you know?
You got divorced at 32--

00:20:20.385 --> 00:20:23.347
-That doesn't mean I wasn't in love.
-You just told me you weren't in love.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.891
No. I actually said I was in love,
but I had doubts.

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.310
Who says yes to getting married

00:20:28.685 --> 00:20:32.397
-and you have doubts?
-This is called culture clash.

00:20:32.481 --> 00:20:35.984
So this is, like, an Indian thing.
I feel you're going back--

00:20:36.068 --> 00:20:37.402
It is. It's a very cultural thing

00:20:37.486 --> 00:20:40.739
to not understand societal pressure
on getting married

00:20:41.031 --> 00:20:42.866
-to somebody you've dated--
-No, I get that.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:45.869
My parents had an arranged marriage.
They met on their wedding day.

00:20:45.953 --> 00:20:47.871
You've to marry a man for life,
not your mom.

00:20:47.955 --> 00:20:49.831
-So you're saying--
-But you lied to a man.

00:20:49.915 --> 00:20:51.625
-You lied to a man.
-I did.

00:20:51.708 --> 00:20:52.793
-You did.
-I was thinking--

00:20:52.876 --> 00:20:54.795
-As long as you admit that.
-Yeah, of course!

00:20:54.878 --> 00:20:58.507
I'm not saying I did the right thing.
I thought, "You know what? It's not--"

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:01.218
-You ruined eight years of your life.
-I didn't ruin it. I learned--

00:21:01.301 --> 00:21:03.720
You totally ruined it.
You lied to him and yourself.

00:21:03.804 --> 00:21:05.722
-Correct. I never said I didn't do it.
-You agreed

00:21:05.806 --> 00:21:07.349
to spend your life with someone

00:21:07.432 --> 00:21:10.102
in front of friends and family,
and it was a complete lie.

00:21:10.185 --> 00:21:11.311
How could I ever trust you?

00:21:13.563 --> 00:21:15.315
How would anyone ever trust you?

00:21:17.985 --> 00:21:18.860
[sighs]

00:21:21.405 --> 00:21:24.157
Look, I don't want you to be upset.
If you're gonna go on a date,

00:21:24.241 --> 00:21:27.369
and you're gonna talk to me
or any guy in New York City,

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:29.288
I feel like they'll ask
the same questions,

00:21:29.371 --> 00:21:31.623
because you wanna know
the true, honest answers.

00:21:31.707 --> 00:21:33.500
You wanna know somebody from their core.

00:21:33.583 --> 00:21:35.877
And if you can't handle that
and gonna get defensive,

00:21:36.336 --> 00:21:37.796
-I'm asking you--
-Obviously,

00:21:37.879 --> 00:21:40.340
we're never gonna see each other
again after this,

00:21:40.674 --> 00:21:42.217
so I'm trying to understand,

00:21:42.301 --> 00:21:45.637
-what you're trying to ask from me?
-All right. This was fun. I'm over this.

00:21:46.054 --> 00:21:47.180
Good. Please.

00:21:47.681 --> 00:21:49.057
By all means,

00:21:49.141 --> 00:21:50.100
exit.

00:21:56.481 --> 00:21:57.816
[exhales sharply]

00:22:02.195 --> 00:22:03.155
[exhales deeply]

00:22:24.009 --> 00:22:25.218
[Gurki sighs wearily]

00:22:29.681 --> 00:22:30.682
[sniffs]

00:22:51.703 --> 00:22:52.954
[Manny] Oh, man. What a night.

00:22:53.955 --> 00:22:56.458
-Oh, that was a great night.
-'Cause you're such a goofball.

00:22:56.541 --> 00:22:58.251
-It's great.
-Thanks.

00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:00.796
-[chuckles] You're like, "I try."
-I mean,

00:23:00.879 --> 00:23:03.465
goofball wasn't what I was going for,
but I'll take it.

00:23:04.132 --> 00:23:07.761
-No, it's good.
-I was expecting suave, elegant, funny,

00:23:07.844 --> 00:23:09.513
-but I guess--
-Yeah. That too.

00:23:09.596 --> 00:23:11.807
I'm kidding. Goofball works.

00:23:12.224 --> 00:23:13.934
I am a little bit of a goofball.

00:23:19.314 --> 00:23:20.357
Is this my stop?

00:23:20.816 --> 00:23:22.025
-[man] Yes.
-Cool.

00:23:22.734 --> 00:23:25.028
-Well...
-This has been a pleasure.

00:23:25.112 --> 00:23:28.740
-Let me get your Instagram.
-Can I get your number if that's okay?

00:23:29.616 --> 00:23:30.992
-Sure.
-A little more official.

00:23:31.952 --> 00:23:33.995
-I'd like that if you wanna put it in.
-Okay.

00:23:35.831 --> 00:23:37.457
What are you doing this weekend?

00:23:37.541 --> 00:23:40.335
I am probably sleeping in this weekend.

00:23:45.632 --> 00:23:48.009
[Gurki] I loved meeting you.
You're amazing.

00:23:48.093 --> 00:23:48.927
No, you.

00:23:49.553 --> 00:23:51.972
"You hang up."
"No, you hang up."

00:23:53.265 --> 00:23:54.474
Thank you so much again.

00:23:54.558 --> 00:23:55.684
-See you.
-Have a good night,

00:23:55.892 --> 00:23:57.477
morning, night, morning.

00:23:57.727 --> 00:23:58.645
